          {
            "type": "output_text",
            "text": "**Foreign investor flows into the KOSPI (Korea Composite Stock Price Index) have been highly volatile in 2026**, with large swings in net buying/selling that significantly influence the index, especially given that foreigners own roughly 30–39% of the KOSPI’s market capitalization (higher in key names like Samsung Electronics).[[1]](https://seoulclosingbell.com/how-foreign-investor-flows-move-the-kospi/)\n\n### Recent Trends (as of early September 2026)\n- **Most recent data (Sept 2, 2026)**: Foreign investors were net sellers of approximately **₩1.92 trillion** on the KOSPI. This was ₩1.42 trillion more selling than the previous day.[[2]](https://caelion.dev/en/indicators/kospi_foreign_net_buy/)\n- **Weekly patterns (August 2026 examples)**: Flows fluctuated sharply. In the week of Aug 24–28, foreigners were net sellers of **₩8.32 trillion**. Earlier in the month they showed net buying (e.g., +₩6.55T in Aug 10–14) interspersed with selling weeks.[[1]](https://seoulclosingbell.com/how-foreign-investor-flows-move-the-kospi/)\n- **Mid-2026 context**: There were periods of heavy net selling (e.g., over ₩75–96 trillion in stretches from May–July, including a notable ~₩28 trillion sold over eight sessions in late June/early July that contributed to sharp KOSPI drops). However, foreigners also returned as strong net buyers at times, such as ₩2.12 trillion on June 12, helping push the index above 8,000.[[3]](https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/economy/others/20260612/foreign-investors-return-to-kospi-buying-powering-rally-above-8000)\n\n### Longer-Term and Q2 2026 Highlights\n- In Q2 2026, foreign holdings of Korean securities surged dramatically to a record **US$2.33 trillion** (up $859.3 billion quarter-over-quarter), driven largely by a 67.8% KOSPI rally that boosted the valuation of existing positions. Total foreign investment in South Korea hit **$3.02 trillion**.[[4]](https://en.yna.co.kr/view/AEN20260820003100320)\n- Foreign ownership of KOSPI stocks has fluctuated but reached multi-year highs at points (e.g., around 37–39% in early 2026), even during net selling periods, due to strong market gains in large-cap stocks (particularly semiconductors).[[5]](https://www.koreaherald.com/article/10677789)\n- Key buyers by nationality in various periods included investors from the **US, Ireland, UK**, and others, with flows often concentrated in chips, IT, and heavy industry.[[6]](https://biz.chosun.com/en/en-finance/2025/10/28/C726CXMCI5FYPCKN4RUIVZPXJ4/)\n\n### Why Foreign Flows Matter for KOSPI\nForeign investors are a major swing factor because of their large ownership stake. Persistent net buying tends to support rallies (often tied to global risk appetite, FX rates, and semiconductor/AI themes), while heavy selling can pressure the index, though domestic institutions and individuals have offset some outflows in 2026.[[1]](https://seoulclosingbell.com/how-foreign-investor-flows-move-the-kospi/)\n\nFor the most up-to-date daily or weekly figures, sources like the Korea Exchange (KRX), Naver Finance, or datasets such as the open GitHub repository tracking per-stock foreign flows are commonly referenced.[[7]](https://github.com/james-brand/korea-market-data) Data can vary slightly depending on whether it includes alternative trading platforms like Nextrade.",
